# 25. Multiple Beans of the Same Type

A common problem occurs when Spring finds **more than one Bean of the same type**.

For example, suppose we have:

```text
Payment
   ├── CardPayment
   └── UpiPayment
```

Both classes implement the same interface:

```java
public interface Payment {
    void pay();
}
```

### Card Payment

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via Card");
    }
}
```

### UPI Payment

```java
@Component
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via UPI");
    }
}
```

Now suppose another Bean requires:

```java
Payment payment;
```

Spring finds:

```text
Payment
   ↑
   ├── CardPayment Bean
   └── UpiPayment Bean
```

Spring now has a problem:

> **Which Bean should be injected?**

This creates an ambiguity.

---

# 26. The Ambiguity Problem

Consider:

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    private Payment payment;

    public OrderService(Payment payment) {
        this.payment = payment;
    }
}
```

Spring tries to create:

```text
OrderService
      |
      ↓
   Payment
```

But there are two possible Beans:

```text
Payment
  |
  ├── CardPayment
  |
  └── UpiPayment
```

Spring cannot automatically decide which implementation should be injected.

Therefore, we need to tell Spring which Bean should be preferred.

There are two important solutions:

1. `@Primary`
2. `@Qualifier`

---

# 27. `@Primary`

## What is `@Primary`?

`@Primary` tells Spring:

> **If multiple Beans of the same type are available, prefer this Bean by default.**

Example:

```java
@Component
@Primary
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via UPI");
    }
}
```

Now:

```text
Payment
  |
  ├── CardPayment
  |
  └── UpiPayment ← @Primary
```

If Spring needs a `Payment` Bean and there is no more specific instruction, it selects:

```text
UpiPayment
```

---

# 28. Why Do We Need `@Primary`?

Suppose:

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {
}
```

and:

```java
@Component
@Primary
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {
}
```

Now Spring has:

```text
2 Payment Beans
       |
       ↓
Which one?
       |
       ↓
@Primary
       |
       ↓
UpiPayment
```

So the dependency can be resolved without ambiguity.

---

# 29. Important Point About `@Primary`

`@Primary` means:

> **Give this Bean priority when multiple candidates are available.**

It does **not** mean:

> There can only be one Bean.

Both Beans still exist:

```text
CardPayment Bean
UpiPayment Bean
```

But `UpiPayment` gets preference when Spring needs to select a `Payment` Bean.

---

# 30. Example with `@Primary`

### Payment Interface

```java
public interface Payment {

    void pay();
}
```

### Card Payment

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via Card");
    }
}
```

### UPI Payment

```java
@Component
@Primary
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via UPI");
    }
}
```

### Order Service

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    private Payment payment;

    public OrderService(Payment payment) {
        this.payment = payment;
    }

    public void placeOrder() {
        payment.pay();
        System.out.println("Order Placed");
    }
}
```

Spring sees:

```text
OrderService
     |
     ↓
Payment required
     |
     ├── CardPayment
     |
     └── UpiPayment
             ↑
          @Primary
```

Therefore:

```text
UpiPayment
     ↓
pay()
     ↓
Paying via UPI
```

The lecture demonstrates this exact idea using Card Payment and UPI Payment.

---

# 31. `@Qualifier`

`@Primary` is useful when we have a **default/preferred Bean**.

But sometimes we don't want a default.

We want to explicitly say:

> **Inject this particular Bean.**

For this, we use:

```java
@Qualifier
```

---

# 32. Using `@Qualifier`

Suppose:

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {
}
```

and:

```java
@Component
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {
}
```

Now both are candidates.

We can explicitly specify:

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    private Payment payment;

    public OrderService(
            @Qualifier("upiPayment") Payment payment) {

        this.payment = payment;
    }
}
```

Now Spring knows exactly which Bean to inject:

```text
OrderService
     |
     ↓
@Qualifier("upiPayment")
     |
     ↓
UpiPayment
```

---

# 33. Bean Name with `@Component`

By default, Spring generally creates the Bean name from the class name with the first letter converted to lowercase.

Example:

```java
@Component
public class UpiPayment {
}
```

Bean name:

```text
upiPayment
```

Similarly:

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment {
}
```

Bean name:

```text
cardPayment
```

Therefore:

```java
@Qualifier("upiPayment")
```

refers to the `UpiPayment` Bean.

---

# 34. `@Qualifier` Example

### Card Payment

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via Card");
    }
}
```

### UPI Payment

```java
@Component
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via UPI");
    }
}
```

### Order Service

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    private Payment payment;

    public OrderService(
            @Qualifier("upiPayment") Payment payment) {

        this.payment = payment;
    }

    public void placeOrder() {
        payment.pay();
        System.out.println("Order Placed");
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
Paying via UPI
Order Placed
```

---

# 35. `@Primary` vs `@Qualifier`

| `@Primary` | `@Qualifier` |
|---|---|
| Defines a preferred/default Bean | Explicitly selects a specific Bean |
| Used on Bean class | Usually used at injection point |
| Useful when one implementation should be preferred | Useful when a particular implementation is required |
| Spring chooses it automatically | Developer explicitly tells Spring which Bean to use |
| Can act as a default | More specific than `@Primary` |

### Simple Memory Trick

```text
@Primary
    ↓
"Prefer this one"

@Qualifier
    ↓
"Use this exact one"
```

---

# 36. What Happens if Both Are Used?

Suppose:

```java
@Component
@Primary
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {
}
```

and:

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {
}
```

Normally:

```java
Payment payment;
```

will receive:

```text
UpiPayment
```

But if we explicitly specify:

```java
@Qualifier("cardPayment")
Payment payment
```

then Spring selects:

```text
CardPayment
```

because `@Qualifier` gives a more specific instruction.

Conceptually:

```text
Multiple Beans
      |
      ↓
Is there a Qualifier?
      |
   YES ─────→ Use specified Bean
      |
     NO
      ↓
Is there a Primary?
      |
   YES ─────→ Use Primary Bean
      |
     NO
      ↓
Ambiguity
```

---

# 37. Complete Dependency Resolution Flow

Suppose we have:

```text
Payment
  |
  ├── CardPayment
  └── UpiPayment
```

And:

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    public OrderService(Payment payment) {
    }
}
```

Spring needs to resolve:

```text
Payment
```

It finds two Beans.

### Case 1 — No `@Primary`, No `@Qualifier`

```text
CardPayment
UpiPayment
     ↓
Ambiguous
     ↓
Spring cannot decide
```

---

### Case 2 — `@Primary`

```text
CardPayment
UpiPayment ← @Primary
     ↓
UpiPayment selected
```

---

### Case 3 — `@Qualifier`

```java
@Qualifier("cardPayment")
Payment payment
```

Result:

```text
CardPayment selected
```

---

# 38. Why `@Qualifier` Is Useful

Imagine an application has:

```text
Payment
  ├── CardPayment
  ├── UpiPayment
  ├── NetBankingPayment
  └── WalletPayment
```

Different services may require different payment methods.

For example:

```text
OrderService
     ↓
UPI Payment
```

while:

```text
SubscriptionService
     ↓
Card Payment
```

Using `@Qualifier`, each service can explicitly choose its required implementation.

---

# 39. `@Primary` as a Default Strategy

If your application normally uses UPI:

```java
@Component
@Primary
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {
}
```

Then most places can simply request:

```java
Payment payment;
```

and Spring will select:

```text
UpiPayment
```

Only places that require something different need:

```java
@Qualifier("cardPayment")
```

This makes `@Primary` useful for establishing a default implementation.

---

# 40. Bean Creation and Dependency Resolution

Spring's overall process can now be understood as:

```text
1. Start Application
        ↓
2. Create ApplicationContext
        ↓
3. Read Configuration
        ↓
4. Perform Component Scanning
        ↓
5. Find @Component classes
        ↓
6. Create Bean Definitions
        ↓
7. Create Bean Objects
        ↓
8. Resolve Dependencies
        ↓
9. If multiple candidates:
        |
        ├── @Qualifier → Select specified Bean
        |
        ├── @Primary → Select preferred Bean
        |
        └── Neither → Ambiguous
        ↓
10. Inject Dependencies
        ↓
11. Beans become ready
```

---

# 41. Bean Definition

Before creating an actual Bean object, Spring maintains information about the Bean.

This information is called:

> **Bean Definition**

A Bean Definition contains metadata/information about how Spring should create and manage a Bean.

Conceptually:

```text
Bean Definition
      |
      ├── Class information
      ├── Bean name
      ├── Scope
      ├── Dependencies
      ├── Configuration
      └── Other metadata
```

Spring keeps Bean Definitions for the Beans it needs to manage.

---

# 42. Why Does Spring Need Bean Definitions?

A natural question is:

> Why doesn't Spring directly create the objects?

Because Spring needs information about how each Bean should be handled before creating and managing it.

Conceptually:

```text
Class
  ↓
Metadata
  ↓
Bean Definition
  ↓
Bean Creation
  ↓
Dependency Injection
  ↓
Bean Management
```

This allows Spring to manage the complete lifecycle of the Bean.

---

# 43. Object vs Bean vs Bean Definition

These three concepts should not be confused.

### Object

An ordinary Java object:

```java
PaymentService paymentService =
        new PaymentService();
```

---

### Bean

An object managed by Spring:

```text
Spring Container
      ↓
PaymentService Bean
```

---

### Bean Definition

Information that tells Spring how to create/manage that Bean:

```text
Bean Definition
      ↓
PaymentService metadata/configuration
      ↓
Spring creates Bean
```

### Remember

```text
Bean Definition ≠ Bean

Bean Definition → Instructions/metadata

Bean → Actual managed object
```

---

# 44. Reflection + Bean Creation

The Java Reflection API becomes important because Spring needs class metadata.

Conceptually:

```text
@Component Class
       ↓
Reflection
       ↓
Class Metadata
       ↓
Spring understands class
       ↓
Bean Definition
       ↓
Bean Creation
```

Reflection allows Spring to inspect things such as:

- Class name
- Constructors
- Methods
- Fields
- Annotations
- Other class metadata

This is why the lecture introduces Reflection before explaining Spring's object-management process.

---

# 45. Complete Spring Core Architecture

The complete mental model is:

```text
                    Spring Application
                           |
                           ↓
                  ApplicationContext
                           |
                           ↓
                     IoC Container
                           |
                           ↓
                  Component Scanning
                           |
                           ↓
                    Find Components
                           |
              ┌────────────┴────────────┐
              ↓                         ↓
       OrderService               PaymentService
        @Component                  @Component
              |                         |
              └────────────┬────────────┘
                           ↓
                    Bean Definitions
                           |
                           ↓
                     Bean Creation
                           |
                           ↓
                  Dependency Resolution
                           |
                ┌──────────┴──────────┐
                ↓                     ↓
           @Qualifier              @Primary
                |                     |
                └──────────┬──────────┘
                           ↓
                  Dependency Injection
                           |
                           ↓
                    Managed Beans
```

---

# 46. Full Example

## Payment Interface

```java
public interface Payment {

    void pay();
}
```

---

## CardPayment

```java
@Component
public class CardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via Card");
    }
}
```

---

## UpiPayment

```java
@Component
@Primary
public class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paying via UPI");
    }
}
```

---

## OrderService

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    private final Payment payment;

    public OrderService(Payment payment) {
        this.payment = payment;
    }

    public void placeOrder() {
        payment.pay();
        System.out.println("Order Placed");
    }
}
```

Because `UpiPayment` is `@Primary`:

```text
OrderService
      |
      ↓
Payment
      |
      ├── CardPayment
      |
      └── UpiPayment ← @Primary
                         ↓
                    Injected
```

---

# 47. Using `@Qualifier` Instead

If we want Card Payment specifically:

```java
@Component
public class OrderService {

    private final Payment payment;

    public OrderService(
            @Qualifier("cardPayment") Payment payment) {

        this.payment = payment;
    }

    public void placeOrder() {
        payment.pay();
    }
}
```

Now:

```text
@Qualifier("cardPayment")
          ↓
CardPayment
          ↓
Injected into OrderService
```

---

# 48. Important Spring Core Flow — Final Revision

Remember this sequence:

```text
                 Spring Application
                         ↓
                ApplicationContext
                         ↓
                   IoC Container
                         ↓
                Configuration Class
                         ↓
                  @ComponentScan
                         ↓
                Find @Component
                         ↓
                  Bean Definition
                         ↓
                   Create Beans
                         ↓
              Resolve Dependencies
                         ↓
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             ↓                       ↓
        @Qualifier               @Primary
             ↓                       ↓
     Exact Bean selected      Preferred Bean
             └───────────┬───────────┘
                         ↓
                 Dependency Injection
                         ↓
                  Managed Bean Ready
```

---

# 49. Most Important Annotations

| Annotation | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `@Component` | Marks a class as a Spring-managed component |
| `@Configuration` | Marks a configuration class |
| `@ComponentScan` | Tells Spring where to find components |
| `@Primary` | Gives priority to a Bean when multiple candidates exist |
| `@Qualifier` | Selects a specific Bean |
| `@Autowired` | Requests dependency injection |

---

# 50. Final Interview Revision

### What is IoC?

Transfer of control of object creation and management from the developer to Spring.

### What is an IoC Container?

The Spring container that creates, configures, wires, and manages Beans.

### What is a Bean?

An object managed by the Spring IoC Container.

### What is `ApplicationContext`?

A Spring interface representing the application container.

### What is `@Component`?

It marks a class so Spring can discover and manage it as a Bean.

### What is `@ComponentScan`?

It tells Spring where to search for component classes.

### What is `@Configuration`?

It marks a class as a source of Spring configuration.

### What is `@Primary`?

It tells Spring:

```text
"Prefer this Bean when multiple candidates exist."
```

### What is `@Qualifier`?

It tells Spring:

```text
"Use this specific Bean."
```

### Difference between `@Primary` and `@Qualifier`

```text
@Primary
   ↓
Default / preferred choice

@Qualifier
   ↓
Explicit / specific choice
```

### What is Reflection?

A Java mechanism that allows runtime inspection of class metadata.

### What is Bean Definition?

Metadata/information Spring uses to create and manage a Bean.

---

# 51. One-Line Memory Map

```text
@Component
    ↓
@ComponentScan
    ↓
@Configuration
    ↓
ApplicationContext
    ↓
IoC Container
    ↓
Bean Definition
    ↓
Bean Creation
    ↓
Dependency Resolution
    ↓
@Qualifier / @Primary
    ↓
Dependency Injection
    ↓
Managed Bean
```

---

# 52. Final Takeaway

The entire Spring Core concept can be summarized as:

> **You define your classes and tell Spring which classes it should manage. Spring's IoC Container discovers those classes, creates their Bean Definitions, creates the Beans, resolves their dependencies, injects the required dependencies, and manages the Beans throughout their lifecycle.**

When multiple implementations of the same type exist:

```text
@Primary
   → choose the preferred/default implementation

@Qualifier
   → choose a specific implementation
```

This is the core foundation on which the higher-level Spring and Spring Boot features are built.